            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>BetterHelp — Best for Online Therapy</h2>

        <p>If you're looking for structured, professional mental health support — not just meditation — BetterHelp is the most comprehensive option available in Australia. It connects you with Australian-licensed therapists via video, phone, or text chat, with the platform handling matching, scheduling, and billing.</p>

        <p>BetterHelp's strength is access. In Australia, wait times for a psychologist through the public system can stretch to months. BetterHelp can often match you with a therapist within 48 hours. All therapists are registered and vetted before joining the platform. Pricing is around A$60–80 per week (billed monthly at A$240–320/month), which is comparable to out-of-pocket costs for a private psychologist — but without the gap fees or travel time.</p>

        <p>The platform supports ongoing therapy, not crisis support. If you're in a mental health emergency, contact <strong>Lifeline 13 11 14</strong> or <strong>000</strong>. BetterHelp is not designed for crisis situations.</p>

        <p>BetterHelp is particularly relevant if you've tried meditation apps and found them insufficient — because apps alone can't replace a genuine therapeutic relationship. You can read our full breakdown in our <a href="/blog/best-online-therapy-platforms-australia">guide to online therapy platforms in Australia</a>.</p>

        <h2>Headspace — Best for Building a Meditation Habit</h2>

        <p>Headspace is the most well-known meditation app for a reason: it works. Its structured course system makes it genuinely easy for beginners to start meditating and stick with it. The app uses short, guided sessions (typically 10 minutes) with a friendly, non-spiritual approach that won't alienate sceptics.</p>

        <p>Research supports its effectiveness — a 2022 study in <em>JMIR Mental Health</em> found that Headspace users who completed 10 or more sessions showed significant reductions in self-reported stress and anxiety. The app's gamification (streaks, milestone badges, progress tracking) genuinely helps with habit formation, which is where most people fail with meditation.</p>

        <p>At A$69.99/year (or A$14.99/month), it's in the middle of the range for meditation apps. The free Starter Pack is enough to get a feel for the approach before committing. Australian users report no accessibility issues — pricing is in AUD and all content is available.</p>

        <p>Headspace's limitation: it's better as an entry point than a long-term practice. Once you've worked through the core courses, the library can feel thin. But as a first meditation app, it's solid. We compared it directly against Calm in our <a href="/blog/calm-vs-headspace-meditation-apps">Headspace vs Calm comparison</a>.</p>

        <h2>Calm — Best for Sleep and General Wellbeing</h2>

        <p>Calm built its reputation on sleep — and it remains the standout app for this purpose. The signature "Sleep Stories" (celebrity-narrated bedtime meditations) are genuinely effective for people who struggle to switch off at night. If sleep is your primary concern, Calm is worth the subscription alone.</p>

        <p>Beyond sleep, Calm offers daily meditation sessions, breathing exercises, body scans, and masterclasses. The "Daily Calm" feature delivers a new 10-minute session each morning, which is a simple but effective way to maintain a practice. Content quality is consistently high — voice talent is excellent, and the app feels polished throughout.</p>

        <p>At A$59.99/year (or A$14.99/month), it's priced similarly to Headspace. The free 7-day trial is sufficient to test the sleep content. Calm's weakness is the same as Headspace's: it lacks the depth to support long-term, committed practitioners. But for general stress reduction and sleep improvement, it's excellent.</p>

        <h2>What's Up — Best Free Mental Health App</h2>

        <p>What's Up is a genuinely impressive free app that uses evidence-based CBT (Cognitive Behavioural Therapy) techniques to help users manage stress, anxiety, and depression. It includes tools like a thought journal (based on CBT's cognitive reframing approach), grounding exercises, coping strategies, and community forums.</p>

        <p>The fact that it's completely free — with no upselling to a premium tier to unlock the useful features — makes What's Up stand out. There are ads, but they're not aggressive. For a completely free option that's grounded in real therapeutic technique (not just guided meditations), this is the best thing available.</p>

        <p>The limitation: it doesn't offer connection to a real therapist, and the interface is less polished than Headspace or Calm. But as a self-help tool, it's remarkably solid for a free product.</p>

        <h2>MindDoc — Best for Mood Tracking</h2>

        <p>MindDoc is primarily a mood-tracking app — designed to help you understand your emotional patterns over time. You log your mood daily (takes about 30 seconds), answer brief questions about sleep, stress, and activities, and the app builds a picture of your mental wellbeing over weeks and months.</p>

        <p>This is genuinely useful for people who want to understand what affects their mood — and for bringing data to a therapy session. Seeing your mood patterns mapped out can help both you and your therapist identify triggers and patterns you might otherwise miss.</p>

        <p>The free version allows unlimited mood tracking. The paid version (A$34.99/year) adds insights, access to CBT courses, and the ability to export reports to share with a health professional. MindDoc is not a replacement for therapy or crisis support — it's a tracking and awareness tool.</p>

        <h2>Insight Timer — Best Free Meditation App</h2>

        <p>Insight Timer is the anomaly in this list: genuinely free with no catches. You get unlimited access to thousands of guided meditations — including full courses — without paying. The quality varies (it's a platform hosting many teachers rather than a curated app), but the best teachers (Tara Brach, Jack Kornfield, Sharon Salzberg) are genuinely world-class.</p>

        <p>The social features are unique — you can see how many people worldwide are meditating alongside you at any given moment, which adds a sense of community that other apps lack. The paid version (A$54.99/year) adds courses, workshops, and offline downloads.</p>

        <p>For serious meditators who don't want to pay, Insight Timer is the obvious choice. For casual users who want a more curated, polished experience, Headspace or Calm still feel more intentional. We covered it alongside other meditation apps in our <a href="/blog/best-meditation-apps-australia">roundup of the best meditation apps in Australia</a>.</p>

        <h2>The Research — Do Mental Health Apps Actually Work?</h2>

        <p>The evidence varies by app type. Meditation apps (Headspace, Calm, Insight Timer) have the strongest research backing for stress and anxiety reduction. A 2020 meta-analysis in <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em> reviewed 18 studies and found moderate improvements in anxiety and depression from mindfulness meditation programmes. CBT-based apps (What's Up, some BetterHelp features) have robust clinical evidence for anxiety and depression.</p>

        <p>Mood-tracking apps like MindDoc have less direct evidence for improving outcomes — but the data they collect can be clinically useful when shared with a therapist or GP. The act of daily mood logging itself may have a mild therapeutic effect through increased self-awareness.</p>

        <p>The key caveat: <strong>apps work best as a complement to professional support, not a replacement</strong>. For mild to moderate stress and anxiety, apps can be an effective and affordable first step. For moderate to severe mental health conditions, they should be used alongside — not instead of — professional diagnosis and treatment.</p>

        <h2>How to Choose the Right Mental Health App</h2>

        <p>Here's the quick version:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Need structured therapy with a real professional:</strong> BetterHelp — but only for non-crisis, ongoing support.</li>
          <li><strong>Want to start meditating and build a habit:</strong> Headspace's free pack, then upgrade if you stick with it.</li>
          <li><strong>Primary concern is sleep:</strong> Calm's Sleep Stories are worth the subscription.</li>
          <li><strong>On a tight budget:</strong> What's Up (for CBT-based self-help) or Insight Timer (for free guided meditation).</li>
          <li><strong>Want to understand your mood patterns:</strong> MindDoc's free tier for tracking, paid tier for insights.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>All of these apps have free content you can try today. The hardest step isn't finding the right app — it's starting. If you've been putting it off, pick one from this list and download it now.</p>

